\fs96 \cf2 Deep dive - Instance initialization\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo tracks two kinds of initialization for structs: fieldwise and logical.\
Fieldwise initialization means the struct's storage holds valid values for each field. Logical initialization means the instance as a whole is in a valid, usable state.\
Understanding the difference between these two forms of initialization is essential for working with structs correctly.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 The basics\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You create struct instances by calling the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  constructor:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 Person\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  name\cf5 :\cf14  String\cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  age\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 __init__\cf5 (out\cf14  self\cf5 ,\cf14  name\cf5 :\cf14  String\cf5 ,\cf14  age\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf14 name \cf5 =\cf14  name\cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf14 age \cf5 =\cf14  age\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 main\cf5 ():\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  me \cf5 =\cf14  Person\cf5 (\cf16 "Alice"\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf17 30\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Calling 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Person("Alice", 30)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is syntactic sugar for calling the constructor directly:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  me\cf5 :\cf14  Person\cb1 \
\cb13 me \cf5 =\cf14  Person\cf5 .\cf14 __init__\cf5 (\cf16 "Alice"\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf17 30\cf5 )\cf14  \cf6 # Identical\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 When constructing a Person, the compiler first allocates stack space for the instance 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 me
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , then 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  initializes that space.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Fieldwise vs logical initialization\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Initializing a struct by assigning values directly to its fields may populate the data, but it does not make the instance usable:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 Person\cf5 (\cf14 Writable\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  age\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  height\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 ...\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 main\cf5 ():\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  me\cf5 :\cf14  Person\cb1 \
\cb13     me\cf5 .\cf14 age \cf5 =\cf14  \cf17 25\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     me\cf5 .\cf14 height \cf5 =\cf14  \cf17 6\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 me\cf5 )\cf14   \cf6 # Error\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf6 # error: 'me' used with all fields manually initialized\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf6 # but without calling an '__init__' method\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 In this example, all fields contain valid values, but the instance is still not considered initialized.\
Mojo tracks two distinct initialization states:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Fieldwise initialization means each field contains valid data and the instance's storage has been populated.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Logical initialization means the instance as a whole is valid and its 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method has run.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Fieldwise initialization alone isn't enough. The compiler requires logical initialization before an instance can be used:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  me \cf5 =\cf14  Person\cf5 (\cf17 25\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf17 6\cf5 )\cf14   \cf6 # Now OK: __init__() ran\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 print(\cf14 me\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Calling 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  makes an instance logically initialized. This process isn't just about assigning data. It ensures that any required initialization logic ran and that the instance is safe to use.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Inside 
\fs56 \cf10 \cb12 __init__()
\fs64 \cf2 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Within 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , the incoming 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  instance is considered logically initialized, but its fields are still uninitialized:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 __init__\cf5 (out\cf14  self\cf5 ,\cf14  name\cf5 :\cf14  String\cf5 ,\cf14  age\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf6 # At this point:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf6 # - Logically initialized (self is valid as an instance)\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf6 # - Fieldwise uninitialized (fields have no values yet)\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     self\cf5 .\cf14 name \cf5 =\cf14  name\cb1 \
\cb13     self\cf5 .\cf14 age \cf5 =\cf14  age\cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf6 # Now both logically and fieldwise initialized\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This distinction is intentional. Entering 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  establishes the instance itself, but it's your responsibility to populate its fields.\
You must initialize every field in 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 __init__\cf5 (out\cf14  self\cf5 ,\cf14  name\cf5 :\cf14  String\cf5 ,\cf14  age\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     self\cf5 .\cf14 name \cf5 =\cf14  name\cb1 \
\cb13     \cf6 # Error: field 'age' not initialized in __init__\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  signature doesn't have to mirror the struct's fields. You can use parameters, constants, or external values to initialize those fields:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb13 # Parameters can be used to initialize fields\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf14 \cb13 self\cf5 .\cf14 _store \cf5 =\cf14  List\cf5 [\cf14 T\cf5 ](\cf14 capacity\cf5 =\cf14 Count\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb13 # Constants can be used to initialize fields\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf14 \cb13 self\cf5 .\cf14 string \cf5 =\cf14  \cf16 ""\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb13 # External values can be used to initialize fields\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb13 from\cf14  std\cf5 .\cf14 math \cf5 import\cf14  pi\cb1 \
\cb13 self\cf5 .\cf14 default_angle \cf5 =\cf14  pi \cf5 /\cf14  \cf17 2.0\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13 self\cf5 .\cf14 uuid \cf5 =\cf14  MyUUIDImplementation\cf5 .\cf14 uuid\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You can't call methods until all fields are initialized:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 __init__\cf5 (out\cf14  self\cf5 ,\cf14  name\cf5 :\cf14  String\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     self\cf5 .\cf14 greet\cf5 ()\cf14       \cf6 # Error: self not fully initialized\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     self\cf5 .\cf14 name \cf5 =\cf14  name\cb1 \
\cb13     self\cf5 .\cf14 greet\cf5 ()\cf14       \cf6 # OK: all fields initialized\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Field initialization is restricted to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  methods. Regular functions can't initialize individual fields of an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 out
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  argument, but 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  methods can.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Moving from fields\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Moving a value out of a field deinitializes that field:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 Person\cf5 (\cf14 Writable\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  name\cf5 :\cf14  String\cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  age\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 __init__\cf5 (out\cf14  self\cf5 ,\cf14  name\cf5 :\cf14  String\cf5 ,\cf14  age\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf14 name \cf5 =\cf14  name\cb1 \
\cb13         self\cf5 .\cf14 age \cf5 =\cf14  age\cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 main\cf5 ():\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  me \cf5 =\cf14  Person\cf5 (\cf16 "Connor"\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf17 25\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  name_owner \cf5 =\cf14  me\cf5 .\cf14 name\cf5 ^\cf14   \cf6 # Move value from me.name.\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf14 me\cf5 )\cf14      \cf6 # Error: use of uninitialized value 'me'\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 After the move, the instance is still logically initialized, but it's only partially fieldwise initialized. Mojo doesn't allow using instances with uninitialized fields.\
To make the instance usable again, you must reinitialize the moved-from field:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  me \cf5 =\cf14  Person\cf5 (\cf16 "Connor"\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf17 25\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 var\cf14  name_owner \cf5 =\cf14  me\cf5 .\cf14 name\cf5 ^\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13 me\cf5 .\cf14 name \cf5 =\cf14  \cf16 "John"\cf14       \cf6 # Reinitialize field\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 print(\cf14 me\cf5 )\cf14              \cf6 # OK\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13 use_value\cf5 (\cf14 name_owner\cf5 )\cf14  \cf6 # Now, use name_owner\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Any moved field requires a new value before you can use the instance again. Even if all fields are moved, the instance itself remains logically initialized.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Mojo's default destructor\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is Mojo's default destructor (or deinitializer). While it runs, the instance remains logically initialized, and it becomes logically deinitialized when the method completes:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 @fieldwise_init\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 Contact\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  name\cf5 :\cf14  String\cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  email\cf5 :\cf14  String\cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 __del__\cf5 (deinit\cf14  self\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf6 # `self` is initialized\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 print(\cf16 "destroying contact"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf6 # `self is deinitialized\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 main\cf5 ():\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  me \cf5 =\cf14  Contact\cf5 (\cf16 "Alice"\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf16 "alice@example.com"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf6 # Last use of `me` (and end of scope)\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf6 # Prints "destroying contact" along with a warning\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf6 # since the instance was never used after assignment.\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Destructors and moving fields\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Destructors are the only place where you can safely move values out of an instance's fields without having to reinitialize the field before its next use.\
This special rule isn't about where the code lives. It's about guarantees:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 __del__\cf5 (deinit\cf14  self\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  name \cf5 =\cf14  self\cf5 .\cf14 name\cf5 ^\cf14   \cf6 # OK: can take ownership of fields\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The compiler knows that destructors like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  are the final use of an instance. Because of this, it allows you to move values out of fields without reinitializing them.\
This ensures that fields are only moved out of struct instances when the compiler can guarantee the instance is at the end of its lifetime.\
Like instances, struct fields use ASAP destruction within deinit methods. For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 struct S:\cb1 \
\cb13    var a: String\cb1 \
\cb13    var b: String\cb1 \
\
\cb13    def __del__(deinit self):\cb1 \
\cb13        # Mojo calls a.__del__() here.\cb1 \
\cb13        use(b)\cb1 \
\cb13        # Mojo calls b.__del__() here.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 The 
\fs56 \cf10 \cb12 deinit
\fs64 \cf2 \cb1  convention\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A destructor's secret sauce is the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 deinit
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  convention. Because of this, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 deinit self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  works in named destructors as well as 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 Parent\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 consume\cf5 (deinit\cf14  self\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf6 # `consume` is a named destructor\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf6 # Can move values out of fields here too\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 When you use the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 deinit self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  convention in a method, the compiler recognizes the method as a destructor and lets you move a field without reinitializing it:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 Parent\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 consume\cf5 (deinit\cf14  self\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf16 """valid destructor"""\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 var\cf14  name \cf5 =\cf14  self\cf5 .\cf14 name\cf5 ^\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 print(\cf16 "Name:"\cf5 ,\cf14  name\cf5 )\cf14  \cf6 # Prints name\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Any struct method that uses 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 deinit self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  as its first argument is a destructor. As a destructor, you can use this 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 consume
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/death/#explicitly-destroyed-types"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 explicit destruction types}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Explicit and implicit destructors\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method is the core of implicit destruction types. It's Mojo's default destructor and works hand in hand with 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ImplicitlyDestructible
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , a trait that all structs conform to by default. The compiler calls 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  automatically when it detects the final use of an implicitly destructible struct value.\
As the name suggests, explicit destruction requires you to call a destructor. An explicitly destroyed type must define at least one custom destructor, such as the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 consume()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method shown in the previous section. The compiler make sure that the final use of an explicitly destroyed value will be a call to one of its custom destructors.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Using 
\fs42 \cf10 \cb12 deinit
\fs48 \cf2 \cb1  with other arguments\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 deinit
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  convention isn't limited to 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . You can write methods and functions that destruct other instances:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf14 \cb13 struct Pair:\cb1 \
\cb13     def destroy_other(self, deinit other: Self):\cb1 \
\cb13         # Can take from fields of `other` here\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Like 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 deinit self
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 deinit
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  convention in this example tells the compiler that 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 other
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is tagged for destruction.\
Using 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 deinit
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  means 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 other
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is logically deinitialized at the end of the method. Because of this, it's safe to move values out of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 other
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , since the instance's lifetime is guaranteed to complete.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Normal methods and moving fields\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In ordinary methods, you can't move values out of a struct's fields without reinitializing them:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 @fieldwise_init\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf15 MyStruct\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  name\cf5 :\cf14  String\cb1 \
\
\cb13     \cf5 def\cf14  \cf15 move_field\cf5 (mut\cf14  self\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf5 var\cf14  new_name\cf5 :\cf14  String\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 var\cf14  name \cf5 =\cf14  self\cf5 .\cf14 name\cf5 ^\cf14  \cf6 # Error\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf6 # error: 'self.name' is uninitialized at the implicit return\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf6 #        from this function\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13         \cf5 print(\cf16 "Name:"\cf5 ,\cf14  name\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 main\cf5 ():\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  instance \cf5 =\cf14  MyStruct\cf5 (\cf16 "Ken"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     instance\cf5 .\cf14 move_field\cf5 (\cf16 "Scott"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf16 "Name:"\cf5 ,\cf14  instance\cf5 .\cf14 name\cf5 )\cf14  \cf6 # Prints: "Name: Scott"\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 To move safely, reinitialize the instance's fields before the end of scope. The following update fixes the bug by reinitializing the field:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 move_field\cf5 (mut\cf14  self\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf5 var\cf14  new_name\cf5 :\cf14  String\cf5 ):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  name \cf5 =\cf14  self\cf5 .\cf14 name\cf5 ^\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 print(\cf16 "Name:"\cf5 ,\cf14  name\cf5 )\cf14   \cf6 # Prints name\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     self\cf5 .\cf14 name \cf5 =\cf14  new_name\cf5 ^\cf14  \cf6 # reinitialize the name field\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Destroying instances\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Unless you opt into {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/death/#explicitly-destroyed-types"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 explicit destructors}}, Mojo uses implicit destruction. Both explicit and implicit destructors use ASAP ("As Soon As Possible") destruction:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf15 example\cf5 ():\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 var\cf14  x \cf5 =\cf14  SomeType\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     use\cf5 (\cf14 x\cf5 )\cf14  \cf6 # last use in scope\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf6 # x destroyed here, immediately after last use\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cb13     more_code\cf5 ()\cf14   \cf6 # x is already gone\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf18 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf19 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 With ASAP, Mojo destroys instances immediately after their last use, not at the end of scope. This can happen even in the middle of an expression.\
This differs from many languages with compiler-managed lifecycles, where destruction happens at end of scope. Mojo's ASAP destruction enables better tail call optimization, but it also means destructors can run earlier than you might expect.\
Mojo also supports explicitly-destroyed types, which require you to make an explicit call to a destructor method. For more information, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/lifecycle/death/#explicitly-destroyed-types"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 Explicitly-destroyed types}}.\
Mojo tracks the lifetimes of values in your code. It knows when you move out of a value using the transfer operator (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 ^
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ), or when you call a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 deinit
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  method that explicitly destroys it. If the compiler finds a live value that hasn't otherwise been cleaned up, it uses the implicit destructor (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__(deinit self...)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ) to destroy it.\
If a type doesn't have an implicit destructor because it requires explicit destruction, the compiler emits an error, as indicated by the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 @explicit_destroy
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  decorator.\
While the compiler checks that a destructor is called before an instance goes out of scope, you're responsible for choosing when that happens. The key differences are:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Destruction doesn't automatically happen at the point of last use.\
\ls5\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The explicit destructor isn't named 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __del__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Object initialization summary\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Creating, using, and destroying instances in Mojo follows a consistent set of rules based on initialization state.\
Create instances by calling 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . You must initialize every field in the initializer, especially if you plan to use the instance within the method scope. You can't call methods on a struct unless all its fields are initialized.\
You must logically initialize an instance before using it, which means calling the initializer. You must also initialize fields before accessing them. Partially initialized instances can't be used in many contexts, including method calls and return values.\
For implicitly destroyed structs, the compiler inserts a destructor call after the instance's last use. For explicitly destroyed values, you must call a destructor before the instance goes out of scope. Destruction is compiler-checked, but you choose when it happens. The value's final use in scope must be that explicit destructor call.\
Field operations are also constrained. Fieldwise initialization must happen inside the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 __init__()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  constructor. You can move values out of a field only when the compiler can prove that the instance won't be used again, or that the field will be reinitialized before the end of a normal method.\
This split between logical and fieldwise initialization gives Mojo fine-grained control over instance lifetimes, while ensuring that initialization, use, and destruction remain safe and explicit.\
\pard\pardeftab720\ri960\partightenfactor0
